    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 2027 Hyundai Bayon Finally Looks More Like a Box and Less Like an Egg\nChinese Brand AITO Plans 2027 European Launch\n9 photos\nPhoto: AITO\nAITO now has a date for its European launch. Fall 2027 is when the Chinese brand intends to launch in the European space, yet no details regarding the lineup or pricing strategy have been offered thus far. Controlled by the SERES Group, the company is expected to bring a selection of electric-only and extended-range electric vehicles to the Old Continent.\nThe latest edition of the Paris Motor Show gave AITO the chance to put a few models on display in front of potential customers, with the company showing off the 9, 8, 7, and 6 sport utility vehicles. 9 is the largest of the bunch, measuring just over 5.2 meters (206 inches) between the front and rear bumpers and 3.1 meters (122 inches) between the front and rear axles.\nBased on these dimensions, the 9 would have to prove itself against the likes of the Mercedes-Benz GLS, BMW X7, and the Audi brand's Q9. The powertrain strategy is to give customers a choice between electric and extended-range electric powertrains, with the latter being a setup in which the combustion engine is there solely to generate electricity rather than driving the wheels.\nEREVs were designed specifically for the kind of customer who wants electric driving without any charging-related compromises. On longer journeys where the charging infrastructure may not be good enough for a trouble-free driving experience, the onboard generator saves the day as long as there is dinosaur juice in the fuel tank.\nAs noted earlier, the European lineup remains a work in progress. Displaying four vehicles in the French capital is not the same as confirming those four models for sale in the union, and AITO has not named the initially targeted markets either. For the time being, the Chinese newcomer is approaching groups willing to represent it.\nPhoto: AITO The dealership network is especially important for customers in this particular segment, for premium SUV buyers expect somewhere to take their vehicles for servicing, a healthy supply of replacement parts in the event of a fender bender, and technicians capable of repairing all hardware or software issues. If you ask me, aftersales concerns me just as much as quality when it comes to Chinese vehicles. This and other concerns are why the dealership network matters so much.\nAITO's pitch for Europe leans heavily on software, which is hardly attractive to anyone who simply wants a dependable vehicle. Gifted with Huawei goodies, including the Harmony operating system and choice advanced driver assistance features, AITO models lean a lot more into all things technology than their supposed German rivals from BMW, Mercedes-Benz, and Audi .\nBefore selling prospective customers the technology promise, AITO has to adapt its driver-assistance system for local roads and Europe's specific driving conditions. At press time, the company is working toward type approval and five-star Euro NCAP safety ratings under the 2026 testing protocols.\nAITO claims to have accumulated 40 billion kilometers (24.9 billion miles) to date, of which more than 10.6 billion clicks (6.6 billion miles) were driven with some degree of driver assistance. Manufacturer-reported figures mean little, though, especially when prospective customers from Europe don't know how the automaker's systems behave on their own roads.\nAnother hurdle AITO has to consider is the import bill. Its Chinese-built vehicles would face an EU tariff of 30 percent under current rules, and that levy alone convinced a bunch of Chinese automakers to invest big money in European production facilities. Given that the brand currently lacks a high-volume model that would justify building one, European production doesn't seem likely by decade's end.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nAito Europe China 2026 Paris Motor Show EV EREV\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Ferrari, Lamborghini, Porsche: It's Time To Start Thinking About Rockets",
